5D-5L index score and EQ-5D-VAS score indicate patient-reported health status. P value for continuous variables is from a Kruskal-Wallis test, and for categorical variables from a χ 2 test. b Patient reported. c Other included patients who reported their race as American Indian or Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander, or other. d Calculated as weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. e Background CV risk categories are mutually exclusive. Impact of Semaglutide on First Hospital Admission The comparison between the semaglutide and placebo groups for first admission to hospital is summarized in Figure 1 and Figure 2 . Patients treated with semaglutide were less likely to experience any hospital admission (33.4% vs 36.7%; HR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.84-0.93; P < .001), a hospital admission recorded as a serious adverse event (30.3% vs 33.4%; HR, 0.88; 95% CI, 0.84-0.93; P < .001), and an elective hospitalization (6.3% vs 7.3%; HR, 0.86; 95% CI, 0.77-0.97; P = .01). The mean number of days per hospitalization did not significantly differ between the semaglutide and placebo groups (13.0 vs 13.2 days; P = .76). Median number of days per hospitalization were 7.0 days for both treatment groups. In terms of admissions for serious adverse events classified according to different indications, patients treated with semaglutide were less likely to have a hospital admission for cardiac (10.4% vs 12.3%; HR, 0.83; 95% CI, 0.76-0.91; P < .001), infection (6.9% vs 8.0%; HR, 0.85; 95% CI, 0.76-0.95; P = .004), and respiratory (1.9% vs 2.9%; HR, 0.66; 95% CI, 0.54-0.80; P < .001) indications. The difference between semaglutide and placebo groups regarding hospitalization for COVID-19 (2.5% vs 2.9%; HR, 0.87; 95% CI, 0.72-1.04; P = .12) was not statistically significant. Patients treated with semaglutide were also less likely to be admitted for a surgical or medical procedure (4.6% vs 5.7%; HR, 0.81; 95% CI, 0.71-0.92; P = .001). There were numerically more hospitalizations for hepatobiliary indications in the semaglutide group compared to placebo (115 vs 93); however, the difference was not statistically significant ( P = .13). After 3 years of treatment, the lower rate of first hospitalization in the semaglutide group compared with the placebo group (31.2% vs 34.4%) resulted in an absolute risk reduction of 3.2%, and the number of patients needed to treat with semaglutide to prevent a hospitalization was 31 patients. Subgroup analyses highlighted that the benefit of semaglutide treatment on the rate of first hospital admissions did not significantly differ with clinical characteristics ( Table 2 ; eFigure 2 in Supplement 2 ). Figure 1. Cumulative Incidence Curve for Time From Randomization to First Hospital Admission Cumulative incidence of time to first hospitalization in patients treated with semaglutide or placebo using the Aalen-Johansen estimator. Figure 2. First Hospital Admissions by Type and Indication First hospital admissions in patients treated with semaglutide or placebo, including all admissions, hospitalizations recorded as serious adverse events (SAEs), and elective hospitalizations, followed by recorded indications for hospitalizations for SAEs. HR indicates hazard ratio. Table 2. Analysis of First Hospitalization Admission by Subgroups a Parameter No. of events/No. of patients (%) HR (95% CI) Interaction P value Semaglutide (n = 8803) Placebo (n = 8801) Sex Female 786/2448 (32.1) 830/2424 (34.2) 0.93 (0.84-1.02) .30 Male 2152/6355 (33.9) 2397/6377 (37.6) 0.87 (0.82-0.92) Age, y <55 570/2057 (27.7) 638/2094 (30.5) 0.89 (0.80-1.00) >.99 55 to <65 1041/3387 (30.7) 1128/3338 (33.8) 0.88 (0.81-0.96) 65 to <75 1002/2656 (37.7) 1128/2706 (41.7) 0.88 (0.81-0.96) ≥75 325/703 (46.2) 333/663 (50.2) 0.87 (0.75-1.01) BMI b <30 817/2555 (32.0) 895/2469 (36.2) 0.85 (0.77-0.94) .63 30 to <35 1244/3693 (33.7) 1362/3781 (36.0) 0.92 (0.85-0.99) 35 to <40 573/1687 (34.0) 614/1659 (37.0) 0.90 (0.80-1.01) 40 to <45 202/579 (34.9