umber of hospital admissions and days spent in hospital among patients treated with semaglutide or placebo are summarized in Figure 3 and in eTable 1 in Supplement 2 . A total of 11 287 hospital admissions occurred throughout the trial (5345 in the semaglutide group and 5942 in the placebo group). Baseline clinical characteristics of patients, stratified according to the number of hospital admissions, are summarized in eTable 2 in Supplement 2 . Overall, patients experiencing more than 1 hospital admission were, on average, older (median [IQR] age, 64.0 [57.0-70.0] years vs 63.0 [56.0-69.0] years vs 60.0 [54.0-67.0] years; P < .001); were more likely to be male (75.0% vs 72.9% vs 71.5%; P = .001) and White (86.6% vs 84.6% vs 83.3%; P < .001); had higher measures of body weight (median [IQR] body weight, 95.7 [86.0-109.0] kg vs 94.5 [85.2-106.0] kg vs 93.8 [84.0-105.7] kg; P < .001), BMI (median [IQR] BMI, 32.4 [29.7-36.2] vs 32.3 [29.7-35.8] vs 32.0 [29.7-35.6]; P = .01), and waist circumference (median [IQR] circumference, 111.8 [104.0-120.0] vs 110.0 [103.0-119.0] vs 109.0 [102.0-118.0]; P < .001); and were more likely to have a history of polyvascular disease (13.4% vs 9.9% vs 6.4%; P < .001), hypertension (85.7% vs 82.8% vs 80.5%; P < .001), atrial fibrillation (15.3% vs 10.6% vs 6.8%; P < .001), obstructive sleep apnea (20.7% vs 16.0% vs 12.6%; P < .001), and heart failure (30.6% vs 25.1% vs 22.7%; P < .001) when compared with those with 1 or no hospital admissions, respectively. The number of total hospitalizations during the trial was lower in the semaglutide group compared with the placebo group for all indications (18.3 vs 20.4 admissions per 100 patient-years; MR, 0.90; 95% CI, 0.85-0.95; P < .001) and for serious adverse events (15.2 vs 17.1 admissions per 100 patient-years; MR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.84-0.94; P < .001). The number of days hospitalized per 100 patient-years was lower in the semaglutide group compared with the placebo group for all hospitalizations (157.2 vs 176.2 days; RR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.82-0.98; P = .01) and hospitalizations for serious adverse events (137.6 vs 153.9 days; RR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.81-0.98; P = .02). Subgroup analyses did not provide evidence of statistical heterogeneity of the benefit of semaglutide on total hospitalizations (eTable 3 and eFigure 3 in Supplement 2 ). Similar MRs in support of the benefit of treatment with semaglutide on total hospitalizations were observed between patients with and without serious adverse events attributable to COVID-19 infection. Figure 3. Total Hospital Admissions and Days in Hospital Total number of hospital admissions (A and B) and number of days in hospital (C and D) per 100 patient-years in patients treated with semaglutide or placebo, including all hospitalizations (A and C) and admissions recorded as serious adverse events (SAEs) (B and D). The total number of hospitalizations was analyzed using a Ghosh-Lin model. Number of hospitalization days was analyzed using a negative binomial regression model. MR indicates mean ratio; RR, rate ratio. Discussion This prespecified analysis of the SELECT randomized clinical trial reported that administration of semaglutide to patients with established CVD, overweight or obesity, and no history of diabetes was associated with an 11% lower rate of admission to hospital for any indication. These patients are at high risk of multiple hospital admissions during a long clinical outcomes trial, and semaglutide was associated with a reduction in the total number of hospitalizations by 10% and reduction in cumulative days spent in hospital by 11%. This translated to an absolute difference in the percentage of patients having any hospitalization of 3.3% and 4360 fewer days spent in hospital in patients treated with semaglutide compared with placebo. Reductions in hospitalization for a range of indications, including cardiac, infection, and respiratory causes, in addition to a decrease in admission for surgical and medical procedures, were observed in the semaglutide group. These findings extend the previously reported benefits of semaglutide on MACE to a broader range of clinical and health care system outcomes. It is well recognized that adiposity is an important risk factor for hospitalization for a range of clinical conditions. In the UK, obesity is cited as a factor associated with 876 000 hospital admissions annually, of which more than 11 000 are directly attributed to the presence of obesity. 12 With increasing rates of adiposity globally, the number of annual hospital admissions will continue to rise, adding further strain on health care systems and increasing the complexity of managing patients living with overweight or obesity. 13 Furthermore, considerable evidence indicates that the presence of obesity is associated with prolonged durations of hospital admissions 14 , 15 in a range of clinical settings, such as cardiac surgery, 16 , 17 knee and hip replacement, 18 ischemic stroke, 19 and transfers to intensive care units. 20 The presence of obesity was also cited as a prognostic factor for poor outcome during the COVID-19 pandemic. 21 , 22 In SELECT, we reported that there was no significant difference in COVID-19 infection rates by treatment assignment, but there were fewer deaths due to COVID-19 in the semaglutide treatment arm compared with placebo (43 vs 65; HR, 0.66; 95% CI, 0.44-0.96) and lower rates of serious adverse events due to COVID-19 (232 vs 277; P = .04). 6 Despite this, the rate of hospitalization for COVID-19 indications was similar in the treatment groups. Therefore, interventions that promote weight loss in patients with a range of adverse obesity outcomes have the potential to have favorable effects on hospitalization rates, mediated by more than CV risk reduction. The SELECT trial was not a weight management trial, but rather a trial evaluating the impact of a GLP-1 receptor agonist in patients at high CV risk with overweight or obesity on the risk of MACE. The CV benefits reported are likely to reflect a range of metabolic mechanisms of semaglutide. Nevertheless, semaglutide was associated with a greater mean weight loss compared with placebo (−9.39% vs −0.88%), which may confer additional benefits that could have led to a favorable effect on hospitalizations. It is also likely that other effects of semaglutide, including reductions in inflammation and dysglycemia and improvements in immune function, in addition to direct effects of GLP-1 receptor agonists on vascular and other tissues, may have also contributed to this benefit. 23 Additional studies will be required to dissociate the relative contributions of weight loss and other effects on the clinical benefits of incretin hormone receptor therapeutics. These factors may have contributed to the reduction in hospitalization for cardiac, infection, and respiratory causes, in addition to fewer admissions for surgical and medical procedures. In contrast, it is also not surprising that hospitalization due to gastrointestinal or hepatobiliary causes did not differ between the groups, given the higher rates of gastrointestinal intolerance reported with GLP-1 receptor agonists and the known association of weight loss and GLP-1 agonist treatment with some forms of hepatobiliary disease. 24 , 25 These observations highlight the importance of considering the impact of interventions beyond the primary focus of clinical trials. While the primary objective of SELECT was to determine the effect of semaglutide on MACE, it is possible that other clinical benefits may occur. Given the age and concomitant medical history of patients enrolled in CV trials, in addition to the presence of overweight or obesity, interventions may have beneficial effects on other clinical outcomes. Hospital admissions have additional consequences from a health, economic, and social perspective. Given the costs incurred with any hospital admission, 26 which are greater when the hospital stay is longer, the benefits reported in this analysis have the potential to further support the value of semaglutide in this clinical setting to health care systems, as reported in cost-effectiveness analyses. 27 Hospitalization has an adverse effect on a patient’s emotional and psychological well-being, with increased feelings of depression and anxiety, and a negative impact on their ability to cope and adjust. 28 Admissions not only incur considerable cost on health care payers, but also lead to out-of-pocket costs for patients and reductions in employability and total household income in the years following hospitalization. 29 Hospital admissions also have the potential to adversely impact the health of family members, with evidence that when an individual is hospitalized, their partner’s risk of death increases and remains elevated for up to 2 years. 30 Therefore, interventions that favorably impact hospitalizations are likely to have additional benefits. The finding of a reduction in hospitalizations due to specific causes, such as respiratory disease, warrants further exploration of potential mechanisms that may underscore this benefit and identify new opportunities for research in these clinical fields. The finding of a strong degree of benefit in those with severe obesity may be of utility given the clinical and economic implications of hospitalization in this group and the potential need to identify patients with the most cost-effective approach to warrant use. Limitations A number of limitations should be considered with regard to the presented analyses. The findings are based on data reported by the investigator through case report forms that were specifically designed to capture elective hospitalizations and, in most cases, information related to serious adverse events, but forms were not subject to central adjudication. The SELECT trial was conducted during the COVID-19 pandemic, and the impact of this on hospital admissions remains uncertain; however, it is important to note that the beneficial effect of semaglutide on first and total hospitalization rates did not significantly differ between patients with and without COVID-19–attributable serious adverse events. Similarly, it remains to be determined whether other incretin-based therapies or other approaches to weight loss have similar effects on hospital admissions. It should also be acknowledged that the long-term risk of hospitalization decreases following bariatric surgery. 31 Conclusions In summary, this prespecified exploratory analysis of the SELECT randomized clinical trial observed that administration of semaglutide to patients at high CV risk with overweight or obesity, but without diabetes, was associated with a reduction in the risk of hospital admissions, the rate of total hospitalizations, and the total number of days spent in hospital. When combined with the favorable effects of semaglutide on MACE, heart failure, and other important cardiometabolic risk factors (eg, reduced progression to diabetes and increased regression to normoglycemia, improved kidney outcomes, and weight loss), these results highlight the importance of targeting the GLP-1 receptor with semaglutide in patients at high CV risk in the setting of adiposity.\n\n\n=== TABLES (structured; cell boundaries = ' | ') ===\nTABLE Table 1.: Clinical Characteristics at Baseline According to Hospitalization a\nParameter | Semaglutide (n = 8803) | Placebo (n = 8801)\nMedian (IQR) | P value | Median (IQR) | P value\nTotal (n = 8803) | No admission (n = 5865) | Hospital admission (n = 2938) | Total (n = 8801) | No admission (n = 5574) | Hospital admission (n = 3227)\nAge, y | 61.0 (55.0-68.0) | 61.0 (54.0-67.0) | 63.0 (56.0-70.0) | <.001 | 61.0 (55.0-68.0) | 60.0 (54.0-67.0) | 63.0 (56.0-70.0) | <.001\nSex, No. (%) |  |  |  |  |  |  |  | \nFemale | 2448 (27.8) | 1662 (28.3) | 786 (26.8) | .12 | 2424 (27.5) | 1594 (28.6) | 830 (25.7) | .004\nMale | 6355 (72.2) | 4203 (71.7) | 2152 (73.2) | 6377 (72.5) | 398